  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was a study in two very different stores. Port Washington ran hot\u20146 groomers at $489 per groomer-day (4% above baseline), strong traffic (43 transactions, up 39%), and exceptional retail lift (+130%). That's a store executing well across every function. Hicksville, by contrast, showed grooming softness ($380/groomer, down 5% from baseline) despite stronger MTD trends, with add-on attach collapsing from 38% to 31%\u2014a upsell discipline issue you should address directly with that team. Glen Cove remains a capacity problem; zero groomers scheduled for today and tomorrow is leaving money on the table.\n\nAt the portfolio level, Port Washington's MTD shortfall versus DOW-adjusted expectation is real\u2014you're running 8.3% below where calendar mix would predict. That's not holiday noise; it's performance loss, and it's dragging the company down even as year-to-date you're up 37%. Hicksville is the bright spot: up 4.1% versus adjusted expectation and up 19% on appointments month-over-month, suggesting traction there.\n\nThe pattern across Port Washington and Hicksville is inventory discipline\u2014both locations have 20 out of stock, including high-velocity items like bully sticks and treat tables. You have a $318K lapse pool at Port Washington alone; poor inventory converts bookings into lost sales. Port Washington's bather hours yesterday (7.8h, up from 5.6h baseline) are a leading indicator that you can squeeze more throughput\u2014staffing bathers is your short-term lever.\n\n**Capacity & coverage:** Port Washington 6 groomers today + 15 bather hours, Hicksville 5 groomers today + 0 bather hours, Glen Cove 0 groomers today; next 7 days: 58 groomer-days company-wide."
